================================================================================
  1.  INSTALLATION
================================================================================

Add the crate to your Cargo.toml:

  [dependencies]
  cipherbox-pqc-core = "0.7"

Import the parts you need:

  use cipherbox_pqc_core::{
      MlKemKeyPair, MlDsaKeyPair, MlDsa87KeyPair,
      encrypt_private_key, decrypt_private_key,
      wrap_key, unwrap_key,
      wrap_room_key_for_user, unwrap_room_key,
      encapsulate_to, validate_encapsulation_key,
      encapsulate_any, validate_public_key,
      Decapsulator, HybridKeyPair, KemLevel, Suite,
      file, room,
      kem,
  };

Everything is in the crate root except `file` and `room`, which
are modules.

Error handling: every fallible function returns
`Result<T, CipherboxError>`. `CipherboxError` implements
`std::error::Error`, so `?` works in `fn main() -> Result<(),
Box<dyn std::error::Error>>` and in any function returning a
`Result` that can absorb it.


================================================================================
  2.  YOUR FIRST KEY PAIR
================================================================================

ML-KEM-768 (the default):

  let kp = MlKemKeyPair::new()?;

  println!("level:  {}", kp.level().name());          // ML-KEM-768
  println!("ek len: {}", kp.encapsulation_key().len()); // 1184
  println!("dk len: {}", kp.decapsulation_key().len()); // 2400

ML-KEM-1024:

  let kp = MlKemKeyPair::new_with_level(KemLevel::MlKem1024)?;

  println!("ek len: {}", kp.encapsulation_key().len()); // 1568
  println!("dk len: {}", kp.decapsulation_key().len()); // 3168

Export for storage. The seed is 64 bytes and uniquely determines
both halves of the key pair:

  let (dk_seed, ek) = kp.export_keys();
  assert_eq!(dk_seed.len(), 64);
  assert_eq!(ek.len(), 1184);

Import from a stored seed:

  let kp = MlKemKeyPair::from_seed(&dk_seed)?;

  // Or, if you also have the ek and want to verify it matches:
  let kp = MlKemKeyPair::import_keys(&dk_seed, &ek)?;

`from_seed` always succeeds if the seed is 64 bytes. `import_keys`
additionally checks that the `ek` you supplied matches the seed,
and fails if it does not. Use `import_keys` when the `ek` came
from an untrusted source.

To choose the level from the `ek` length:

  let level = KemLevel::from_ek_len(ek.len())
      .ok_or("unrecognised ek length")?;
  let kp = MlKemKeyPair::from_seed_with_level(&dk_seed, level)?;


================================================================================
  3.  ENCAPSULATION AND DECAPSULATION
================================================================================

The basic operation. Sender has the recipient's public key; the
recipient has the private key.

Sender side:

  let recipient_ek = /* 1184 bytes */;

  // Validate before use if the key came from an untrusted source.
  let level = validate_encapsulation_key(recipient_ek)?;

  let (ct, shared_secret_sender) = encapsulate_to(recipient_ek)?;
  // ct is 1088 bytes, shared_secret_sender is 32 bytes.

Recipient side:

  let (ct, shared_secret_recipient) = kp.decapsulate_received(&ct)?;

  assert_eq!(shared_secret_sender, shared_secret_recipient);

`validate_encapsulation_key` performs the FIPS 203 section 7.2
modulus check. If you skip it, you accept any well-formed byte
string as a public key, which is not what you want on the wire.
Call it on every `ek` you receive from a peer.

Decapsulation never fails for a well-formed ciphertext, even if
the ciphertext is wrong. A tampered ciphertext produces an
unrelated, deterministic 32-byte value (implicit rejection). The
only error `decapsulate` returns is for a wrong-length ciphertext.

  // Tampered ciphertext: no error, but a different secret.
  let mut bad = ct.clone();
  bad[0] ^= 1;
  let ss_after_tamper = kp.decapsulate(&bad)?;
  assert_ne!(ss_after_tamper, shared_secret_recipient);

If you only have the seed, you can decapsulate:

  let kp = MlKemKeyPair::from_seed(&dk_seed)?;
  let ss = kp.decapsulate(&ct)?;

If you want to check that a decapsulation key is internally
consistent (embedded ek hash matches the ek):

  let dk = kp.decapsulation_key();
  if !kem::check_dk_level(kp.level(), dk) {
      return Err("decapsulation key failed validation".into());
  }


================================================================================
  4.  SIGNING AND VERIFYING (ML-DSA)
================================================================================

ML-DSA-65 (default):

  let kp = MlDsaKeyPair::new();
  let msg = b"the message";
  let sig = kp.sign(msg);
  assert_eq!(sig.len(), 3309);

  assert!(kp.verify(msg, &sig));
  assert!(!kp.verify(b"not the message", &sig));

ML-DSA-87 (CNSA 2.0 level):

  let kp = MlDsa87KeyPair::new();
  let sig = kp.sign(msg);
  assert_eq!(sig.len(), 4627);

Export and import:

  let (seed, vk) = kp.export_keys();
  assert_eq!(seed.len(), 32);   // signing-key seed
  assert_eq!(vk.len(), 1952);   // ML-DSA-65 verifying key

  let kp = MlDsaKeyPair::import_keys(&seed, &vk)?;

Verifying with only the public key (no private key needed, no
keypair object needed):

  let vk = /* 1952 or 2592 bytes */;
  let ok = ml_dsa_verify(vk, msg, sig);

`ml_dsa_verify` infers the parameter set from the key length.
Returns `false` for anything malformed. Does not panic.


================================================================================
  5.  ENCRYPTING A PRIVATE KEY WITH A PASSPHRASE
================================================================================

Use this to protect a stored key seed with a user passphrase.

  let kp = MlKemKeyPair::new()?;
  let (dk_seed, _ek) = kp.export_keys();

  let encrypted = encrypt_private_key(&dk_seed, "correct horse battery staple")?;
  // Store `encrypted` in a file, a database, wherever.

  // Later:
  let decrypted = decrypt_private_key(&encrypted, "correct horse battery staple")?;
  let kp = MlKemKeyPair::from_seed(&decrypted)?;

Wrong passphrase produces an error:

  match decrypt_private_key(&encrypted, "wrong passphrase") {
      Ok(_)  => unreachable!(),
      Err(e) => eprintln!("could not decrypt: {e}"),
  }

Custom Argon2 parameters (only if you have measured on your
hardware and want to tune):

  let encrypted = encrypt_private_key_with_params(
      &dk_seed,
      "correct horse battery staple",
      131_072,  // m_cost in KiB (128 MiB)
      4,        // t_cost
      1,        // p_cost
  )?;

The parameters are stored in the blob and authenticated, so a
tampered header cannot weaken the parameters. Decrypt does not
need to know what parameters were used.

Bounds: `m_cost <= 512 MiB`, `t_cost in [1, 16]`,
`p_cost in [1, 16]`. Values outside those bounds are rejected at
encrypt time and at decrypt time.

Note on what a passphrase protects against: it protects the blob
at rest. It does not protect against a compromised machine, a
keylogger, or an attacker who observes the passphrase being
typed. Treat it as one layer, not as the only layer.


================================================================================
  6.  WRAPPING A KEY FOR A RECIPIENT
================================================================================

You have a symmetric key (a room key, a file key, anything). You
want to send it to someone who has a KEM public key.

Binary form (self-describing, best for programmatic use):

  let recipient_ek = /* 1184, 1568, 1216, or 1600 bytes */;

  let blob = wrap_key(recipient_ek, b"the-room-key")?;
  // blob is "CBWK" | 0x01 | kem_ct | nonce | aead_ct.

  // On the recipient side:
  let recovered = unwrap_key(&kp, &blob)?;
  assert_eq!(recovered, b"the-room-key");

`wrap_key` accepts either a plain ML-KEM key (1184 or 1568 bytes)
or a hybrid key (1216 or 1600 bytes). The suite is inferred from
the key length. The shared secret is combined with a domain-
separated hash of the ciphertext and the recipient key, so the
wrapped blob is bound to both.

JSON form (best for interop with a frontend or another service):

  let pk_b64 = base64::engine::general_purpose::STANDARD.encode(recipient_ek);

  let json = wrap_room_key_for_user(&pk_b64, "the-room-key")?;
  // {"v":2,"ct":"...","iv":"...","enc_aes":"..."}

  // On the recipient side, parse the JSON:
  let v       = serde_json::from_str::<serde_json::Value>(&json)?;
  let ct_b64  = v["ct"].as_str().ok_or("missing ct")?;
  let iv_b64  = v["iv"].as_str().ok_or("missing iv")?;
  let enc_b64 = v["enc_aes"].as_str().ok_or("missing enc_aes")?;

  let recovered = unwrap_room_key(&kp, 2, ct_b64, iv_b64, enc_b64)?;
  assert_eq!(recovered, "the-room-key");

`unwrap_room_key` takes the version explicitly (`v["v"]` from the
JSON). Pass `1` to decrypt a legacy v1 blob. Do not guess — read
the `v` field.

The recipient side can use a `MlKemKeyPair` or a
`HybridKeyPair`; both implement the `Decapsulator` trait that
`unwrap_key` and `unwrap_room_key` accept.


================================================================================
  7.  ENCRYPTING A LARGE FILE
================================================================================

One-shot API, when the whole plaintext fits in memory:

  let kp = MlKemKeyPair::new()?;

  let plaintext = std::fs::read("input.bin")?;
  let ciphertext = file::encrypt_file(&plaintext, kp.encapsulation_key(), 65536)?;
  std::fs::write("output.cbox", &ciphertext)?;

  // Decrypt:
  let ciphertext = std::fs::read("output.cbox")?;
  let plaintext  = file::decrypt_file(&ciphertext, kp.decapsulation_key())?;
  std::fs::write("recovered.bin", &plaintext)?;

Chunk size: 65536 is the default. The minimum is 64 bytes, the
maximum is 1048576 bytes. Larger chunks are faster; smaller
chunks give finer truncation detection granularity. There is no
correct answer for every workload — pick something between 64 KiB
and 1 MiB and measure.

If you only have the seed:

  let plaintext = file::decrypt_file_with_seed(&ciphertext, &dk_seed)?;

If the file was encrypted to a hybrid public key:

  let plaintext = file::decrypt_file_with_hybrid_secret(&ciphertext, &hybrid.export_secret())?;

Errors are specific. Every failure mode from the format spec has
a distinct error string:

  "bad magic"
  "unsupported version: 0x.."
  "unknown suite id 0x.."
  "chunk_size ... outside allowed range [...]"
  "chunk N failed to authenticate"
  "last chunk failed to authenticate (truncation or wrong key)"
  "incomplete header"
  "file was encrypted for X but the key is Y"
  "file was encrypted for X: a hybrid key is required"

If you get "chunk N failed to authenticate" for N > 0, the file was
tampered with after chunk N-1. If you get "last chunk failed to
authenticate", the file was either truncated or encrypted to a
different key.

Partial plaintext is never written. If `decrypt_file` returns
`Err`, the file you were decrypting is either untouched or you
wrote nothing. On a streaming decryptor, the caller decides
whether to keep the partial output — the crate does not write it
anywhere on its own.


================================================================================
  8.  STREAMING A LARGE FILE
================================================================================

When the file does not fit in memory, use the streaming API. Both
encryptor and decryptor accept arbitrary-sized slices.

Encrypting:

  use std::io::{Read, Write};

  let mut input  = std::fs::File::open("large.bin")?;
  let mut output = std::fs::File::create("large.cbox")?;

  let mut enc = file::FileEncryptor::new(kp.encapsulation_key(), 65536)?;

  let mut in_buf  = vec![0u8; 65536];
  let mut out_buf = Vec::with_capacity(65536 + 32);

  loop {
      let n = input.read(&mut in_buf)?;
      if n == 0 { break; }

      out_buf.clear();
      enc.update(&in_buf[..n], &mut out_buf)?;
      if !out_buf.is_empty() {
          output.write_all(&out_buf)?;
      }
  }

  out_buf.clear();
  enc.finish(&mut out_buf)?;
  output.write_all(&out_buf)?;

Decrypting:

  let mut dec = file::FileDecryptor::new(kp.decapsulation_key())?;
  // Or: FileDecryptor::from_seed(&dk_seed)?
  // Or: FileDecryptor::from_hybrid_secret(&secret)?

  let mut in_buf  = vec![0u8; 65536];
  let mut out_buf = Vec::with_capacity(65536);

  loop {
      let n = input.read(&mut in_buf)?;
      if n == 0 { break; }

      out_buf.clear();
      dec.update(&in_buf[..n], &mut out_buf)?;
      if !out_buf.is_empty() {
          output.write_all(&out_buf)?;
      }
  }

  out_buf.clear();
  dec.finish(&mut out_buf)?;
  output.write_all(&out_buf)?;

The streaming decryptor buffers internally until it has a full
header and at least one full chunk. That means the first `update`
call may produce no output at all — that is normal. The header is
between 5 and 1606 bytes depending on the suite and format
version, so the first output appears after roughly one header
plus one chunk of input has been fed in.

Every possible split point across `update` calls produces the
same ciphertext and the same plaintext. The crate tests this
exhaustively. You do not need to align your reads to chunk
boundaries.

Important: `dec.update` will fail as soon as it detects a
tampered chunk. If you have already written earlier chunks to
your output file, those are on disk. Decide whether to delete
the output file on error, or to write to a temporary file and
rename on success — the crate does not make this decision for you.


================================================================================
  9.  HYBRID KEYS
================================================================================

A hybrid key pair combines X25519 and ML-KEM. It is secure if
either of the two holds.

  let kp = HybridKeyPair::new(KemLevel::MlKem768)?;

  println!("public key: {} bytes", kp.public_key().len()); // 1216

  // Encapsulate to the public key:
  let (ct, ss_sender) = cipherbox_pqc_core::encapsulate_hybrid_to(kp.public_key())?;

  // Decapsulate:
  let ss_recipient = kp.decapsulate(&ct)?;
  assert_eq!(ss_sender, ss_recipient);

Export and import:

  let secret = kp.export_secret();
  // 34 bytes: version | level id | 32-byte seed.
  // Store this encrypted.

  let kp = HybridKeyPair::from_seed(&seed32, level)?;
  let kp = HybridKeyPair::from_secret(&secret)?;

Using hybrid keys with `wrap_key`, `unwrap_key`,
`wrap_room_key_for_user`, and `encrypt_file` works the same way
as with plain ML-KEM keys — the suite is inferred from the
public-key length. A file encrypted to a hybrid key cannot be
decrypted with a plain ML-KEM key of the same level and vice
versa, which the crate enforces at the header check.

To inspect what a given public key is:

  let suite = validate_public_key(public_key)?;
  println!("{}", suite.name());  // "ML-KEM-768", "X25519+ML-KEM-768", etc.


================================================================================
 10.  ROOMS: GROUP MESSAGING FROM A SHARED SECRET
================================================================================

A room is a group whose members all share a secret that is
established out of band — a passphrase, the hash of a photo, a
QR code, or several of these combined. No server-side member
list or invitation is needed.

Creating a room secret:

  use cipherbox_pqc_core::room::{RoomSecret, RoomKdfParams};

  let secret = RoomSecret::derive(
      "team-alpha",                              // context
      &[b"correct horse battery staple"],        // sources
      RoomKdfParams::DEFAULT,
  )?;

  println!("room id: {}", hex(secret.room_id()));

The context separates unrelated rooms that share the same
sources. Use something stable (the room's name, the server's
name, a project label). Do not use something that changes, or
the room id will change.

Joining:

  use cipherbox_pqc_core::room::Room;

  let mut room = Room::join(&secret)?;
  // Each call to Room::join generates a fresh random sender id.

  // Or, if you want a specific id:
  let mut room = Room::join_with_sender_id(&secret, [0x01; 16]);

Sending an unsigned message:

  let envelope = room.seal(b"hello")?;
  // Send `envelope` to the other members over whatever channel.

Receiving:

  let opened = room.open(&envelope, None)?;
  println!("from {}: {}", hex(&opened.sender_id), String::from_utf8_lossy(&opened.plaintext));

Sender id and index:

  The `Opened` struct includes `sender_id` (16 bytes), `epoch`
  (u32), `index` (u32), and `signed` (bool). Use them to
  distinguish senders and to detect replays (the same
  `(sender_id, epoch, index)` should only be opened once per
  room instance).

Signing messages:

  let dsa = MlDsaKeyPair::new();
  let (_, vk) = dsa.export_keys();

  let envelope = room.seal_signed(b"hello", &|m| dsa.sign(m))?;

  // On the receiving side, pass a verifier:
  let verify = |m: &[u8], sig: &[u8]| ml_dsa_verify(&vk, m, sig);
  let opened = room.open(&envelope, Some(&verify))?;

If you pass a verifier to `open`, the message must be signed and
the signature must verify, or `open` returns an error before
touching any ratchet state. If you pass `None`, unsigned and
signed messages both work, and the `signed` field tells you
which one you received.

What a room does and does not give you:

  Gives you:
    - Forward secrecy against compromise of a device, as long as
      the root secret is not also compromised.
    - Replay and reordering detection.
    - Optional sender authentication with ML-DSA.
    - Post-compromise security after a rekey.

  Does NOT give you:
    - Forward secrecy against leak of the root secret. Anyone
      who learns the passphrase can derive epoch 0 and, if they
      also recorded the traffic, decrypt everything up to the
      first rekey. Use `rekey` to close this.
    - Sender authentication without a signature. Without a
      signature, any holder of the root can send a message
      that claims to be from any sender id.


================================================================================
 11.  ROOMS: ADDING A MEMBER (REKEY)
================================================================================

Rekeying moves the room to a new epoch with fresh randomness
that is wrapped to each member's KEM public key. After a rekey,
the room stays secret even if the root secret later leaks.

You need:
  - A member's KEM public key (plain or hybrid, any level, any
    suite — mixed levels are allowed within a rekey).
  - A signer that the other members already trust.

  let bob_public_key = /* 1184, 1568, 1216, or 1600 bytes */;
  let alice_dsa = MlDsaKeyPair::new();
  let (_, alice_vk) = alice_dsa.export_keys();

  let bundle = room.rekey(
      &[bob_public_key],
      &|m| alice_dsa.sign(m),
  )?;

  // `bundle` is a RekeyBundle. Send it to the other members.
  // Or serialize it for the wire:
  let bytes = bundle.to_bytes();

The bundle is not secret — it is signed, and its contents are
encrypted to each member's KEM public key. You can relay it
through an untrusted server.

On the other side:

  let bundle = RekeyBundle::from_bytes(&bytes)?;

  let verify = |m: &[u8], sig: &[u8]| ml_dsa_verify(&alice_vk, m, sig);

  room.apply_rekey(&bundle, &bob_kem_keypair, &verify)?;

`bob_kem_keypair` is the receiving member's key pair — a
`MlKemKeyPair` or a `HybridKeyPair`, both work. The key pair is
used to unwrap the fresh seed from the entry that matches its
public-key fingerprint.

Ordering:

  The bundle's epoch must be exactly one more than the newest
  epoch the receiving member knows. `apply_rekey` rejects
  skipped or replayed bundles. If two members rekey concurrently,
  the relay must serialise them (first one wins).

The member who creates the rekey bundle should not include their
own public key in `member_public_keys`. They already have the
new epoch key from having generated it.

Forgetting old epochs:

  By default the room keeps all epoch keys it has ever seen.
  After a rekey has been delivered to everyone, delete the old
  ones:

  room.forget_epochs_before(new_epoch);

  Messages from forgotten epochs can no longer be decrypted.
  That is what makes old epochs forward-secret. Do not call this
  until you are sure no in-flight messages from the old epoch
  are still needed.


================================================================================
 12.  PERSISTING ROOM STATE
================================================================================

Room state is secret and must be persisted encrypted.

  // Export:
  let state = room.export_state();
  let encrypted = encrypt_private_key(&state, passphrase)?;
  std::fs::write("room.state.enc", &encrypted)?;

  // Import:
  let encrypted = std::fs::read("room.state.enc")?;
  let state     = decrypt_private_key(&encrypted, passphrase)?;
  let mut room  = Room::import_state(&state)?;

State includes:
  - room id
  - sender id
  - current epoch
  - all epoch keys the room has seen
  - the send chain (current send index and chain key)
  - every receive chain, including the skipped-key window

Restoring state restores the ability to open messages from the
same positions as before. Do not open the same envelope in two
different copies of a restored room — one of them will fail
because the chain state has advanced.

If you lose the state, you cannot decrypt any messages from
before the loss, even with the root secret. That is the forward
secrecy property working as designed.


================================================================================
 13.  COMMON MISTAKES
================================================================================

Using a public key without validating it.

  Every `ek` that came from the network should go through
  `validate_encapsulation_key` or `validate_public_key` first.
  Without the check, you accept any well-formed byte string as
  a public key.

Reusing a seed across levels.

  MlKemKeyPair::from_seed(&seed) always produces ML-KEM-768.
  If you want ML-KEM-1024, use from_seed_with_level. There is
  no way to tell from the seed itself.

Mixing `wrap_room_key_for_user` and `unwrap_room_key` versions.

  The JSON has a `v` field. Read it and pass it to
  `unwrap_room_key`. Passing 2 for a legacy blob, or 1 for a v2
  blob, produces a "wrong key or tampered data" error. That is
  by design — the versions are not interchangeable.

Using `MlKemKeyPair::encapsulate` to send to a peer.

  `kp.encapsulate()` uses the key pair's own public key. To send
  to a peer, use `encapsulate_to(peer_ek)`. The method exists
  for tests and for the case where a key pair encapsulates to
  itself.

Ignoring the signature on a rekey bundle.

  `apply_rekey` takes a verifier. Do not pass a verifier that
  always returns true, and do not pass a verifier for a key you
  have not authenticated out of band. The rekey's security
  depends entirely on the trustworthiness of the verifying key.

Opening the same envelope twice.

  The second call fails with "message already received or too
  old." That is correct. If you need at-least-once delivery,
  deduplicate on your side.

Streaming decryptor with output already on disk.

  If you write chunk N to a file, then chunk N+1 fails
  authentication, you have a partial plaintext on disk. The
  crate does not clean it up. Either delete the output on error,
  or write to a temporary file and rename on success.

Skipping `forget_epochs_before` after a rekey.

  Until you call `forget_epochs_before`, every epoch key is
  retained in memory and in any exported state. That defeats
  the forward secrecy that rekey is supposed to provide.
  Call it after all members have confirmed the new epoch.


================================================================================
 14.  WHERE TO GO NEXT
================================================================================

Read README.md section 4 before deploying anything. It describes
the trust barriers (not audited, not ACVP-validated) and the
threat-model boundaries (no constant-time analysis on the
compiled binary) that you need to decide about before using this
crate in production.

Read README.md sections 6, 7, and 8 for the exact wire formats of
the hybrid KEM, the room envelope, and the file format, if you
are implementing a peer in another language.

Run `cargo doc --open` for the full rustdoc, including every
method signature and every type.

Run `cargo test --all-targets` to see the 80-test suite pass on
your machine.

If you have a use case the crate does not cover, the RustCrypto
ml-kem crate is the right choice for ML-KEM and the ml-dsa crate
is the right choice for signatures.
